## Track 1: The Chord Arrives After the Wake

**Story & Context:** At 102 BPM, Saruul opens with a yoochin open fifth struck alone on beat one. Daniel withholds the Rhodes for a dotted eighth, then reveals a major-6/9 harmony. Munkhbayar enters another eighth later with a short fretless slide into the tonic. The sequence becomes the central hook: strike, air, chord, bass. Elena adds a Foam Line flugelhorn sixth only after the yoochin decay is nearly gone. In verse two, Wake Reversal changes the same open-fifth attack into a minor-7 interpretation, turning a familiar gesture suddenly wistful without changing the acoustic figure.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Calm lake surface after a small boat has passed, one narrow wake widening across blue water toward a wooden radio lodge, a folded chord chart on the windowsill. Sparse lyric fragments: “after the wake,” “same two notes,” “different answer,” “the water already moved.”

## Track 2: Room 17 Keeps the Softest Signal

**Story & Context:** At 108 BPM, Nate uses the EP’s tightest groove: compact kick, dry snare, soft sixteenth hats, and shaker. Daniel plays high-register Rhodes major-7 and dominant-9 voicings while Saruul uses short single-note yoochin attacks rather than dyads. Every fourth bar becomes a Dry Dock Bar: Rhodes and guitar disappear, leaving yoochin, fretless bass, drums, and room tone. Munkhbayar and Elena use Mirror Berth throughout the bridge, the bass approaching upward while flugelhorn falls by half-step into each target. The arrangement stays polished but never fully closes the space between parts.

**Visual & Lyrics Prompt:** Small radio-service room numbered 17, one weak green signal lamp glowing above a wooden workbench, yoochin hammers beside a handwritten frequency sheet, lake light entering through faded blue curtains. Sparse lyric fragments: “room seventeen,” “keep the softest signal,” “don’t turn it up,” “I can hear it from here.”

## Track 3: Last Ferry Across a Still Frequency

**Story & Context:** The closer runs at 94 BPM. Saruul begins with widely spaced yoochin sixths and add9 fragments, sometimes leaving almost a full bar between strikes. Daniel uses only Rhodes for the first two minutes; guitar enters later with natural harmonics. Munkhbayar plays the least active bass part on the EP, using held roots and brief slides at phrase endings. Elena saves her longest flugelhorn note for the final chorus, entering as a Foam Line after a fading yoochin minor-sixth interval. In the last minute, the drums reduce to kick, rim, and closed hat. The final Hammer Wake is never harmonically completed: Saruul strikes an open fifth, Daniel leaves the Rhodes silent, and the EP ends on sympathetic string decay and distant room tone.

